Title:
Bypassing Sophos Intercept X (Indirect Syscalls + PEB Walk + KnownDlls Unhooking in Zig)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post details a user-mode evasion chain against Sophos Intercept X by avoiding/neutralizing its hooks on `ntdll.dll` syscall stubs.  
- It explains how EDRs patch `Nt*` stubs (e.g., overwriting prologues with `jmp` into `hmpalert.dll`) and then implements indirect syscalls that jump to unhooked `syscall; ret` gadgets inside `ntdll` instead of calling hooked exports.  
- The author locates `ntdll` without Win32 APIs by walking the TEB/PEB loader lists, then scans `ntdll`’s `.text` for `0F 05 C3` gadgets and a “clean” `ret` to spoof return addresses for stack-walk plausibility.  
- Syscall numbers are resolved via export-table ordering (sorted `Nt*` RVAs) plus a build-specific delta, with a fallback that extracts SSNs from clean stubs when available.  
- A HellsGate/HellDescent-style dispatcher masks SSNs/gadget addresses (XOR) and executes syscalls through randomized gadgets to reduce static signatures.  
- It also references a second stage (“Valak”) that restores a clean `ntdll` from `\\KnownDlls` to overwrite Sophos user-mode hooks inside a beacon process.  
- Useful primarily for red teamers, implant/loader developers, and defenders studying syscall-based evasion and hook resilience.

Technical Focus:
- User-mode EDR hooking of `ntdll` `Nt*` syscall stubs (JMP trampolines into EDR DLLs)
- PEB/TEB walking to resolve module bases without `GetModuleHandle`/imports
- Indirect syscalls via `syscall; ret` gadget harvesting in `ntdll` `.text`
- Syscall number (SSN) resolution via export sorting + delta calibration; stub scanning via `.pdata`
- HellsGate/HellDescent-style syscall dispatch, argument shuffling (`RCX`→`R10`), gadget jumping (`jmp r11`)
- Return-address spoofing and simple in-memory obfuscation (XOR-masked SSNs/pointers)

Use Cases:
- Building loaders/implants that avoid user-mode API hooks on `Nt*` functions
- Researching Sophos Intercept X user-mode instrumentation and hook coverage
- Developing syscall dispatch layers without relying on SysWhispers-generated stubs
- Defensive validation: detecting indirect-syscall gadget jumps and PEB-walk module resolution
- Testing EDR robustness against KnownDlls-based `ntdll` restoration/unhooking

Keywords:
Sophos Intercept X, hmpalert.dll, ntdll.dll, user-mode hooking, syscall stub, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory, NtWriteVirtualMemory, PEB walk, TEB, Ldr InMemoryOrderModuleList, indirect syscalls, syscall;ret gadget, HellsGate, HellDescent, SSN resolution, export table sorting, .pdata, KnownDlls, XOR masking, Sliver beacon, reflective loading